## Introduction
In nature, plants are constantly challenged by potential pathogens. Most exhibit non-host resistance to microbial attacks (Thordal-Christensen, 2003); therefore, disease is the exception rather than the rule. Non-host resistance is due to a lack of recognition between the non-host and the non-pathogen (Hammond-Kosack and Jones, 1996). In all other cases, an interaction takes place between host and pathogen. In gene-forgene systems, R gene-mediated signalling in response to the specific recognition product of a pathogen avr gene leads to the exclusion of the pathogen and to an incompatible interaction (Hammond-Kosack and Jones,1996). Activation of defence reactions in gene-for-gene systems is frequently associated with a hypersensitive response (HR), i.e., the death, within a few hours, of infected host cells (Shirasu and Schulze-Lefert, 2000). However, specific R gene-mediated resistance is only one aspect of plant resistance against pathogens. General elicitors may be released from either the pathogen or the plant during contacts with both pathogens and non-pathogens, and the barriers that are activated in response to these elicitors contribute to resistance towards both types of microbes. Exogenous elicitors, usually low-molecular weight compounds, such as flagellin, LPS, and Pep13 oligopeptide (Côté and Hahn, 1994) are often indispensable to the microbe, and plants are supposed to exploit them during recognition in the same way as animals recognise “pathogen-associated molecular patterns” (PAMPs). PAMPs involved in mammal and insect innate immunity include peptidoglycans, lipoteichoic acid of Grampositive bacteria, and lipopolysaccharides (LPS) of Gram-negative bacteria. However, only a few PAMPs have been fully characterised in terms of structure and biological activities in plants and, when they have, it is mainly in incompatible interactions. This, therefore, leaves open the question of the identity or differences in mechanisms occurring in compatible and incompatible interactions.
In Gram-negative bacteria, LPS certainly play a direct role in interactions with eukaryotic host cells because these complex, amphipathic, and tripartite molecules form the outermost layer of the bacterial cell surface. LPS are constituted by a hydrophobic lipid A, a hetero-oligosaccharide part, and a variable antigen O part of repetitive polysaccharides (Rietschel et al., 1994). Both the lipid A and the hetero-oligosaccharide parts, linked together by 2-keto-3-deoxyoctonate (KDO), are inserted into the outer bacterial membrane, while antigen O is located outside the cell membrane. LPS can induce and/or prime defence reactions, and are also able to prevent the hypersensitive response through activation of induced or acquired resistance in incompatible interactions (Dow et al., 2000). Indeed, accumulation of pathogenesis-related (PR) proteins and enhancement of membrane permeability were observed in leaves of tobacco challenged with LPS of Bulkholderia cepacia (Coventry and Dubery, 2001), and an increase in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production was detected in tobacco cell suspensions treated with LPS of Xanthomonas campestris (Meyer et al., 2001). LPS pre-treatment of pepper tissue also affected the timing of accumulation of coumaroyl tyramine and feruloyl tyramine (Newman et al., 2001, 2002). Finally, infiltration of heat-killed Ralstonia solanacearum into leaves of tobacco prevented the appearance of disease symptoms or the HR when the leaves were subsequently inoculated with living bacteria involved in compatible and incompatible interactions (Graham et al., 1977; Dow et al., 2000). The suppression of HR has also been shown in the pathosystems of pepper or brassica/xanthomonads. In this case, the response is mainly due to the conserved lipid A part of LPS (Newman et al., 1995, 1997). In these pathosystems, priming rather than direct induction of defence reactions by LPS has been recently postulated (Erbs and Newman, 2003).
Plant response to elicitation involves early and later reactions, corresponding to three major steps: recognition, signal transduction, and activation of defence reactions (Kauffman et al., 2000). Early reactions include pH changes in the extracellular medium as a consequence of ionic fluxes (primarily ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , and $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ ), and production of reactive oxygen species. Klement et al. (2003) showed that ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ -fluxes take part in the activation of the NADPH oxidase complex, involved in the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and which may result in membrane damage through lipid peroxidation. ROS also act as signals leading to the activation of phenylpropanoïd and octadecanoïd pathways in plant cells (Dixon and Palva, 1995; Grant and Mansfield, 1999). These pathways are involved in cell wall fortification, production of salicylic acid and of jasmonic acid which act as chemical-signalling messengers and accumulation of antimicrobial compounds like phytoalexins and PR proteins (Kombrink and Somssich, 1995). The oxidation of polyunsaturated fatty acid by lipoxygenases (LOX, structurally related to cytochrome P450) provides the precursors of various oxylipins, another step in the plant response to pathogen attack. Oxylipins have been identified as signalling molecules of local-induced resistance (LIR) and as antimicrobial compounds (Göbel et al., 2001).
The objective of this paper is to compare the induction by LPS from two plant-pathogenic bacteria in the defence reactions in three Solanaceae species, which are representative of compatible and incompatible interactions. More specifically, we intended to answer the following questions: i) are LPS from Pectobacterium atrosepticum $(P a)$ and Pseudomonas corrugata (Psc) able to induce early and late defence mechanisms in different Solanaceae species; and ii) do the defence response profiles depend on the nature of the LPS, plant genotype, and/or type of interactions (compatible or incompatible)?
To this end, we challenged cell suspensions of potato, tomato, and tobacco with LPS extracted from the pectinolytic bacterium Pa, (synonym Erwinia carotovora subsp. atroseptica) and from the non-pectinolytic bacterium Psc. Laminarin, an oligosaccharidic elicitor of defence reactions in tobacco (Klarzynski et al., 2000), was used as a control. The two bacterial species were chosen based on their known host range: Pa is pathogenic to potato tubers and stems (Pérombelon, 2002), while Psc is a tomato pathogen isolated from internal stem rots (Achouak et al., 2000; Sutra et al., 1997). We investigated both early (pH changes in extracellular media and production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ) and later defence reactions (induction of lipoxygenase [LOX] activity). Perception and signalling during induction of defence reactions in tomato cell suspensions were additionally investigated using several inhibitors (A9C for anionic channels, $\mathrm{LaCl}_{3}$ for calcium channels, DPI for NADPH oxidase, staurosporin for protein kinases, and apigenin for MAP kinase), which revealed that the various elicitors affected different steps of the signalling cascade. We showed that LPS and laminarin induce defence reactions in all three plant species, but that these reactions are species-specific rather than interaction-specific. We therefore suggest that LPS-mediated responses depend on host plant species rather than on the type of interaction (compatible or incompatible).
## Materials and Methods
## Bacterial strains and culture conditions
For infection experiments, Pectobacterium atrosepticum (Pa, CFPB 5889, Winslow) and Pseudomonas corrugata ( $P s c,$ CFBP 2431, Kluyver and Van Niel) were grown on King B agar for $24\mathrm{h}$ in the dark at $20^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Cells were suspended in a phosphate buffer saline (PBS) solution, the concentration of the resulting suspension was determined by measuring the optical density at $580\mathrm{nm}$ on a spectrophotometer (UVIKON 100, Kontron, Paris) and the concentration was subsequently adjusted to $10^{8}\mathrm{cfu}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ .
For lipopolysaccharide extraction, $P a$ and Psc were grown for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $20^{\circ}C$ in the dark, in agitated liquid King B medium. Cells were collected by centrifugation ( $15\mathrm{min}$ , $10000{\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}$ , washed three times in PBS, and lyophilised before LPS extraction.
## Plant culture and bacterial infections
Seeds from tobacco (Nicotiana tabacum L. cv. Xanthi) and tomato (Solanum lycopersicum L. cv. Merveille and cv. Marmande) and tubers from potato (Solanum tuberosum L. cv. Bintje and cv. Kerpondy) were grown in a greenhouse ( $16\mathrm{h}$ light, $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 months. Stems were inoculated by driving a plastic pipette cone tip containing $100\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of bacterial suspension calibrated at $10^{8}$ cfu $\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ into the leaf axil. For each bacterial strain, three plants were inoculated at two different sites. The whole experiment was performed twice. The symptoms on stems were observed externally 5 days after inoculation. No or localised necroses were considered typical of incompatible interactions, while expanding necroses were considered typical of compatible interactions.
## Lipopolysaccharide extraction and purification
$\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ were extracted using the hot phenol-water method of Westphal and Jann (1965) with some modifications. One gram of lyophilised bacteria was suspended in $100\mathrm{ml}$ of cold distilled water and sonicated for $20\mathrm{min}$ in hot water at $65^{\circ}C$ , before adding $100\mathrm{ml}$ of cold phenol $90\%(\mathrm{w/v})$ . After a centrifugation $16000\mathrm{g},45\mathrm{min};$ , the water phase was collected. The phenol phase was treated again two times with $100\mathrm{ml}$ of cold distilled water, as described above. The three water phase extracts were combined, dialysed against water and lyophilised. Proteins and nucleic acids were removed from $250\mathrm{mg}$ of crude LPS in $20\mathrm{ml}$ of distilled water by enzymatic treatment with Dnase I, Rnase, and proteinase K, each at $100\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ final concentration, before the extract was dialysed and lyophilised again.
The LPS were purified by gel filtration chromatography through a Sephacryl S 400 HR column ( $600\mathrm{mm}$ long, $16\mathrm{mm}$ diameter, $120\mathrm{ml}$ volume; Amersham Bioscience), with $30\mathrm{mM}$ triethylamine $/10\mathrm{mM}$ EDTA (pH 7) as eluent. The chromatography products were collected as successive fractions of $5\mathrm{ml}$ . Concentrations of total carbohydrates (Chaplin, 1986) and nucleic acids were obtained from reading optical densities at $260\mathrm{nm}$ on a spectrophotometer. An assessment of total proteins (Lowry et al., 1951) in the final preparation confirmed that this preparation contained no protein.
The presence of LPS was identified by determining the amount of KDO (Karkhanis et al., 1978). All LPS-containing fractions were pooled, dialysed against distilled water, lyophilised, and tested for purity using the carbocyanine assay (Johnson, 1993). The carbocyanine assay and SDS-PAGE electrophoresis on this pooled fraction (Fig. 1) confirmed the presence of purified LPS, used subsequently for all experiments.
## Analysis of LPS by SDS-PAGE
LPS preparations were analysed by SDS-PAGE in slab gels ( $4\%$ polyacrylamide in stacking gel and $14\%$ polyacrylamide in resolving gel) with the buffer system described by Laemmli (1970). Samples of $20\upmu\up g$ of purified LPS were mixed with one volume of buffer containing $2\%$ (w/v) SDS, $10\%\mathrm{(v/v)}$ glycerol, $5\%$ (v/v) $\upbeta\cdot$ -mercaptoethanol, $62\mathrm{mM}$ Tris $\left(\mathsf{p H6.8}\right)$ , and $0.003\%$ bromophenol blue. The mixture was then heated to $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $5\mathrm{{min}}$ . Electrophoresis was run at 75 volts (stacking gel) and 150 volts (resolving gel) until the tracking dye was approximately $1\mathrm{cm}$ from the bottom of the gel. Gels were fixed in $40\%\:(\mathrm{v/v)}$ ethanol and $5\%(\mathrm{v/v})$ acetic acid and stained for LPS with silver nitrate (Tsai and Frasch, 1982).
## Elicitor preparation
Laminarin was extracted and purified from the marine brown alga Laminaria digitata by Laboratoires Goëmar as described by Klarzynski et al. (2000).
A concentrated culture filtrate (CCF) of Phytophthora infestans (Mont. de Bary), causal agent of late blight (strain isolated from diseased foliage from potato cv. Bintje grown in 2002 in Ploudaniel, France), was prepared from a 3-week-old culture grown in sterile pea broth. The culture broth was prepared by boiling $125{\mathrm{g}}$ of frozen peas in 1.2 l of ion-exchanged water, discarding the peas and autoclaving the broth for $20\mathrm{min}$ at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Andrivon et al., 2003). The filtrate was obtained by separating the mycelium from the culture broth on $0.45\upmu\mathrm{m}$ Whatman filter paper, dialysed against distilled water, and lyophilised before use. Final concentrations used for inductions were $200\upmu\mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ for both laminarin and CCF.
Inhibition of anionic and calcium channels of NADPH oxidase, protein kinases, and MAP kinases
Anthracene-9-carboxylic acid (A9C), inhibitor of anion channels; lanthanum chloride $\left(\operatorname{LaCl}_{3}\right)$ , inhibitor of calcium channels; diphenylene-iodonium (DPI), inhibitor of NADPH-oxidase; staurosporin, inhibitor of protein kinases; and apigenin, inhibitor of MAP kinases were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (St Quentin Fallavier, France). All were dissolved in dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), except for $\mathrm{LaCl}_{3}$ which was dissolved in water, before use. Inhibitor solutions were added to tomato cell suspensions at final, non-lethal concentrations of $200\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (A9C and $\mathrm{LaCl}_{3}$ ), $40\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (DPI), $2\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (staurosporin), or $80\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (apigenin) $30\mathrm{min}$ prior to addition of the elicitors.
## Plant cell culture and culture conditions
Cell suspensions of Nicotiana tabacum cv. BY (Narváez-Vásquez, J., 1991) were maintained and multiplied in Murashige and Skoog medium (Kalys Biotechnologies, Roubaix, France) as described by Klarzynski et al. (2000). Cell suspensions of Solanum lycopersicum cv. MSK8 were maintained in Murashige and Skoog medium at $\mathrm{pH}6.2$ supplemented with $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{l}^{-1}$ of 1-naphthaleneacetic acid (ANA), $2.3\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{l}^{-1}$ of 6-benzylaminopurine (BAP), $500\mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{l}^{-1}$ of myo-inositol, $30\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{l}^{-1}$ of sucrose and vitamins: thiamine $2\log\ \mathrm{ml^{-1}}$ , nicotinic acid $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ aminobenzoic acid $0.1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ , pyridoxine HCl $0.5\mathrm{mg~ml^{-1}},$ calcium panthothenate $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ , biotine $1\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ , choline chloride $5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ , $\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}01.5625\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ , and ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{MoO}}_{4}$ · $\mathrm{\underline{{{H}_{2}O}}}~15.625~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ .
Friable calluses of Solanum tuberosum cv. Bintje and cv. Kerpondy, produced from microtubers cultured on solid Murashige and Skoog medium supplemented with B5 Gamborg’s vitamins, $2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{l}^{-1}$ NAA and $0.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{l}^{-1}$ BAP, were used to establish stable cell suspension cultures in liquid MS medium buffered at $\mathsf{p H}5.8$ and supplemented with $2\mathrm{mgl^{-1}}$ NAA and $0.5\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{l}^{-1}$ BAP.
Cells were grown at $25^{\circ}C$ in the dark on a rotary shaker $(130\mathrm{rpm})$ , and sub-cultured every week for tobacco and every second week for tomato and potato. Elicitation experiments were performed on the day before sub-culture.
## Extracellular pH variation measurement
Variations of extracellular pH were measured in $10\mathrm{ml}$ aliquots of N. tabacum, S. lycopersicum, and S. tuberosum cv. Bintje and Kerpondy cell suspensions. The pH before elicitation was monitored every 30 s for $15\mathrm{min}$ to ensure that the variations observed after elicitors were added were in fact due to elicitor treatment and not to external factors. LPS (200 or $400\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ final concentration), laminarin $200\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ final concentration), or sterile-distilled water were added to the cell suspensions when stabilisation was reached. Extracellular pH was measured with a $\mathfrak{p H}$ electrode for $20\mathrm{min}$ after elicitor addition, every $5s$ for the first $2\mathrm{min}$ and every $30s$ thereafter. Two independent experiments were performed.
## Oxidative burst measurement
The oxidative burst in cell suspensions was measured in automatic fluorometric assays using $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{C F}$ -DA (Sigma Aldrich, St Quentin Fallavier, France), as described by Rosenkranz et al. (1992) with the following modifications. Cell suspensions were washed and diluted in non-quenching medium $(3\%\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{v}$ sucrose, $10\mathrm{mM}$ Tris HCl $\mathsf{p H}7.5$ , $0.5\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{CaCl_{2}}.$ ). Five $\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of a $10\mathrm{-}\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{C F}$ -DA stock in DMSO were added to $1\mathrm{ml}$ of these suspensions, to obtain a final concentration of $50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ The cell suspensions $(50\upmu\mathrm{l})$ were transferred to the wells of a 96-well black fluorescence plate, to which LPS, laminarin, or CCF at final concentrations of $200\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ were subsequently added. Distilled water was added to control wells. The fluorescence resulting from the oxidative burst produced by the cells was measured continuously for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ on a microplate fluorimeter (Fluoroskan Ascent; Labsystems France SA, Les Ulis, France) at an excitation wavelength of $485\mathrm{nm}$ and emission wavelength of $535\mathrm{nm}$ . Relative fluorescence was plotted over time, typically yielding a curve with an initial increase followed by a plateau. The initial slopes of the curves – reflecting the amount of ROS production in the first moments after contact with elicitors – were calculated by linear regression as the slopes of the lines tangent to the fluorescence curves, and statistically compared between treatments (two independent replicates).
## Lipoxygenase activity measurement
Purified $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ or $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ were added to cell suspensions of tobacco, tomato, and potato cv. Bintje and cv. Kerpondy. Laminarin was used as a positive control. Cells were harvested by filtration and frozen at $-20^{\circ}C$ after 5, 8, 18, and $24\mathrm{h}$ of contact with LPS or with laminarin. LOX activity was determined as described by Bohland et al. (1997), with some modifications. Cells $(300\mathrm{mg})$ were ground in a mortar in the presence of $0.6\mathrm{ml}$ of cold Tris buffer ( $0.1\%$ Triton X-100, 3 mM EDTA, $0.04\%\mathrm{Na_{2}S_{2}O_{5}}$ , and $0.1\%$ polyvinylpyrrolidone, $\mathrm{pH}6.8\dot{.}$ . The homogenate was centrifuged at $10000{\mathrm{g}}$ at $5^{\circ}C$ for 15 min. LOX activity in supernatants was measured at $234\mathrm{nm}$ on a spectrophotometer, using linolenic acid as substrate. The reaction mixture contained $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ of linolenic acid, $0.1\mathrm{{M}}$ Tris Buffer $\left(\mathrm{pH}6.8\right)$ , and $200\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of supernatant-containing enzyme. The increase in absorbance was followed for $15\mathrm{min}$ and the rate of increase was calculated from the initial linear proportion of the curve (initial slope). LOX activity was calculated in nkat/g FW. All experiments were performed twice.
## Statistical tests
All statistical tests were made with the GLM procedure of the SAS 8.1 software (SAS Institute, Cary, NC, USA). When appropriate, means of treatments were compared using the Student Newman-Keuls test $(p=0.05$ ).
## Results
Interaction of phenotypes after infiltration of Pectobacterium atrosepticum (Pa) and Pseudomonas corrugata (Psc) on whole tobacco, tomato, and potato plants
Inoculation with Pa caused small, non-expanding necroses on tobacco stems. In our conditions, no externally visible symptoms were observed after inoculation with Psc. This observation is consistent with previous findings of Devlin and Gustine (1992) and Catara et al. (2002), who reported that Psc caused no external symptoms on tobacco. However, Catara et al. (2002) also showed that all strains of Psc tested, including CFBP 2431, were able to cause internal pith necrosis in tobacco plants. Tobacco can therefore be considered as a susceptible host for Psc, and as a resistant host for Pa. Both bacteria showed compatible interactions with tomato stems. Pa induced extended lesions 5 days after infiltration, leading to plant death within 1– 2 weeks, while Psc caused smaller necrotic lesions. Pa also efficiently infected stems of potato cv. Bintje and Kerpondy, although symptoms were (as expected) more severe on cv. Bintje than on cv. Kerpondy. Meanwhile, Psc caused no visible symptoms on stems of these two cultivars, which is consistent with previous reports of its lack of pathogenicity to potato tubers (Lukezic, 1979). Potato was therefore considered as a susceptible host for $P a$ , and as a resistant host for Psc.
$L P S_{\mathsf{P a}}$ and $L P S_{\mathrm{p_{sc}}}$ induce a strong transitory acidification of the extracellular medium of Solanaceae cell suspensions
The addition of $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ (200 and $400\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ ) to cell suspensions of all three Solanaceae species resulted in a significant, immediate pH drop (0.3 to $1.25\mathrm{pH}$ units), increasing with LPS concentration (Fig. 2). A return to initial $\mathsf{p H}$ (or close to it) was observed 20 to $25\mathrm{{min}}$ after LPS addition; it was faster in tobacco and tomato than in potato cell suspensions (Fig. 2). The same results were observed with $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ (results not shown). By contrast, laminarin $(200\upmu\mathrm{g}\:\mathrm{ml}^{-1})$ induced a significant increase in the extracellular $\mathsf{p H}$ in tobacco ${\mathrm{0.70pH}}$ units) and tomato cells ${\tt D}.54\mathrm{pH}$ units). This alkalinisation was more progressive than the acidification due to $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a},$ reaching a stable plateau $8-10\mathrm{min}$ after challenging with the oligosaccharide. Laminarin caused no significant pH change in cell suspensions of potato cvs. Bintje and Kerpondy (Fig. 2). In tomato, the inhibition of anionic channels by A9C did not impair acidification of the extracellular medium by $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ . By contrast, the inhibition of calcium channels by $\mathrm{LaCl}_{3}$ decreased $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and increased $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ effects. The addition of DPI, staurosporin, and apigenin had no effect on the acidification of extracellular medium by $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ (Table 1).
$L P S_{\mathsf{P a}}$ and $L P S_{\mathrm{p_{sc}}}$ induce no $H_{2}O_{2}$ production in tobacco, tomato, or potato cells
Fluorimetric measurements with the $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{C F D A}$ probe showed that $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ did not induce any significant $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ formation in cells suspensions of tobacco, tomato, and potato, even in media with low quenching activity (Fig. 3). Conversely, laminarin and CCF induced strong $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production in tobacco (Fig. 3). In potato (cv. Bintje) and tomato, laminarin did not induce the formation of reactive oxygen species to any significant extent. CCF induced a low formation of reactive oxygen species in tomato (Fig. 3), which was inhibited when adding A9C, $\mathrm{LaCl}_{3}$ , DPI, or staurosporin, but not apigenin (data not shown). CCF did not induce any significant ROS formation in potato cell suspensions (Fig. 3).
$L P S_{\mathsf{P a}}$ and $L P S_{\mathrm{p_{sc}}}$ activate lipoxygenase activity in tomato, but not in tobacco and potato
In tomato, $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ induced a transitory LOX activation, with a maximum at $15-18{\mathrm{h}}$ post-treatment (Fig. 4). The activation curves over time fitted well with second-degree polynomials $\mathrm{R}^{2}{\geq}0.987,$ , $p<0.005,$ ), showing that the activation was transitory rather than steadily increasing. At each point in time, activation increased with increasing LPS concentration (Fig. 4). $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ was a more effective elicitor of LOX activity than $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}.$ In tobacco, the activation patterns of LOX by LPS were much less intense than in tomato; the LOX activities did not differ between LPSs, and did not differ from those in the water controls, at all times (Student Newman-Keuls test, $p{=}0.05$ ). In both species, laminarin induced high LOX activity. Although this induction was higher in tomato than in tobacco, laminarin proved to be a less effective elicitor than either LPS in tomato, while the opposite was true in tobacco (Figs. 4, 5). In potato cvs. Bintje and Kerpondy, LOX activity was not triggered by either laminarin or the LPS, but increased significantly after addition of CCF to the cell suspensions (Fig. 5). Statistical comparisons of LOX activities measured $18\mathrm{h}$ after treatment with the various elicitors in the different species showed three types of behaviour (Fig. 5). Tomato was the most reactive species, with a strong activation of LOX with all elicitors; in tobacco, the response to laminarin was significant, but $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ induced no significant LOX activity; finally, none of the three elicitors induced a change in LOX activity in potato. In tomato, inhibition of anionic channels by A9C, of calcium channels by $\mathrm{LaCl}_{3},$ , and of protein kinases by staurosporin led to suppression of LOX activity induction by the two LPS and laminarin. DPI and apigenin inhibited LOX induction by $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a},$ but not by $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ or by laminarin (Table 2).
## Discussion
Purified lipopolysaccharides of phytopathogenic bacteria activate early and late defence responses in the Solanaceae
Purified LPS of Pectobacterium atrosepticum and Pseudomonas corrugata induced defence responses in the three Solanaceae species or cultivars tested (tobacco, tomato, and potato). First, we showed that LPS added to cell suspensions caused a strong but transitory drop in the extracellular pH in tobacco cv. BY, tomato cv. MSK8, and potato cvs. Bintje and Kerpondy. These acidifications were not expected, since LPS of Bulkholderia cepacia $(\mathrm{LPS}_{B c e p})$ added to cell cultures of Nicotiana tabacum L. cv. Samsun resulted in an alkalinisation of extracellular medium (Gerber et al., 2004). By itself, the acidity of the LPS solution added cannot explain the large pH drop ${\mathrm{(}}0.3-1.2{\mathrm{~pH~}}$ units) observed in our experiments, as it accounted for a decrease in the pH of the final medium by only $0.03-0.05\mathrm{pH}$ units. The acidification is more likely explained by an efflux of $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ to the extracellular medium, possibly concomitant with an influx of ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ . Indeed, Gerber et al. (2004) showed that $\mathrm{LPS}_{B c e p}$ elicits a $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ influx. These proposals are in accordance with the variations in acidification observed when calcium channels were inhibited in cell suspensions of tomato after addition of $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}.$ Moreover, inhibition of anionic channels did not change this acidification. On the contrary, alkalinisation of the extracellular medium after laminarin was added to cell suspensions of tobacco cv. BY is consistent with previous findings (Klarzynski et al., 2000). This reaction was also observed in tomato, but not in the two potato cvs. Bintje and Kerpondy. This discrepancy points to differences in the response to elicitors according to plant genotypes, rather than to the types of interactions considered.
The slow return to the initial pH after the rapid acidification induced by LPS could be related to utilisation of $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ for production of reactive oxygen species. However, this hypothesis is not confirmed by fluorimetric measurements, which allowed detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production only in tobacco and tomato cells treated with CCF and tobacco cells treated with laminarin, but not in any cells treated with LPS. The biosynthesis of reactive oxygen species can be either dependent or independent of NADPH oxidase (Zhao et al., 2005). In our experiments on tomato cells, addition of DPI before elicitation did alter the oxidative burst induction by laminarin and CCF, pointing to an NADPH oxidase-dependent pathway. Production or lack of an NADPH oxidase-dependent oxidative burst is always calcium and kinase protein-dependent. Regarding of LPS, the evidence available on the oxidative burst in response to elicitation by this molecule is controversial: Dow et al. (2000) showed that none of the many different LPS assayed induced an oxidative burst in soybean cell suspensions, whereas Gerber et al. (2004) observed an oxidative burst triggered by $\mathrm{LPS}_{B c e p}$ in tobacco cells. Induction of an oxidative burst could thus be LPSspecific, as also suggested by the detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in tobacco after application of LPS of Xanthomonas campestris pv. campestris but not of Salmonella typhimurium (Meyer et al., 2001).
Recent evidence has indicated that nitric oxide (NO) is released by plant cells alongside ROS upon challenge with diverse elicitors such as cryptogein (Foissner et al., 2000) and LPS from several bacterial species, including P. atrosepticum and $P.$ carotovorum, and also from pseudomonads (Zeidler et al., 2004). Since the probe used detects ROS, but not directly NO, it would be worthwhile to use a method specifically targeting this compound.
Besides the early acidification observed in all cell suspensions, LPS also increased lipoxygenase activity, a later reaction involved in plant defence against pathogens (Montillet et al., 2002), in tomato, but not in tobacco or potato. In solanaceous plants, oxylipins derived from the 9-LOX pathway are of importance for pathogen defence. Pathogen-induced accumulation of 9-LOX transcripts (and particularly of POTLOX-3 transcripts) was reported in potato infected by P. infestans (Göbel et al., 2003). The pathogen-induced accumulation of the 9- LOX products in potato cells suggests a role for these compounds in defence responses (Göbel et al., 2001, 2002). The lack of LOX induction in potato by LPS was not due to the physiological condition of the cells, since LOX activity could be triggered in these cultures with a crude culture filtrate of the oomycete pathogen Phytophthora infestans, as reported by Göbel et al. (2003). In tomato cells, the strong decrease in LOX induction by LPS and laminarin in the presence of A9C and staurosporin suggest that this induction is dependent on anionic channels and protein kinases. Furthermore, the induction of LOX by $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ seems to depend on the activity of a MAP kinase, itself dependent on the activity of anion channels and calcium channels.
## The nature and dose of lipopolysaccharide influence activation of defence responses
The pH shift and LOX activities triggered by LPS were usually significantly more intense at the higher LPS dose ( $400\upmu\mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ ) than at the lower dose $(200\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathfrak{m}^{-1}.$ ) assayed. This observation of a dose-dependent elicitation of defence responses raises the question of the perception of LPS by plant cells because, contrary to mammalian membranes and LPS-binding proteins (LBP), no direct receptors of LPS have yet been identified in plant cell walls.
The intensity of reactions induced also varied between LPS types. These divergences in LPS-mediated responses might be related to the sugar composition of the O antigen (Bedini et al., 2005), the conformation assumed by the molecule, as is the case in mammalian cells (Bedini et al., 2005; Schromm et al., 2000), the origin of the LPS or the perception of the molecule by the plant. The electrophoregrams clearly showed different patterns between $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c},$ but the nature of these differences remains unexplored. It would thus now be useful to investigate more fully the structures of the two LPS molecules in order to better understand the origin of the differences in the induction of defence reactions.
Activation of defence responses by Solanaceae after LPS contact depends on the plant genotype more than on the compatibility/incompatibility of the interaction
The response patterns of tomato and potato to $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ were very dissimilar, although the interaction of $P a$ with both plants is compatible. The response patterns are also very dissimilar between potato and tobacco with $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c},$ two incompatible interactions. Finally, in potato cvs. Bintje and Kerpondy, the responses to $\mathrm{LPS}_{P a}$ and $\mathrm{LPS}_{P s c}$ were identical, although $P a$ is a potato pathogen and $P s c$ a non-pathogen. It therefore appears that activation of defence reactions following LPS contact depends on the plant genotype, rather than on the type of interaction. Most papers dealing with the implication of LPS on activation of plant defences are made on incompatible or symbiotic reactions and results are sometimes very different from those found with compatible reactions (extracellular pH measurement) (Coventry and Dubery, 2001; Gerber et al., 2004; Newman et al., 1995). In our work, all plants responded by acidification after LPS contact, with only quantitative differences. Therefore, all three species appear to perceive the LPS molecules. However, subsequent response (LOX activation) was clearly different between species. This suggests that the differences we observed could most likely result from plantspecific activation/inactivation of signalling cascades rather than from differences in the perception per se.
